<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adalard of Corbie</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 751–827</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adalard of Corbie was a Frankish abbot and statesman, a cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. Abbot of Corbie in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picardy">Picardy</a>, he served as adviser to Charlemagne's son Pippin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, was exiled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> in 814 and restored in 821. In 822 he founded the abbey of Corvey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> with his brother Wala.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adalard-of-corbie.jpg" alt="Adalard of Corbie" width="320" height="550" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bycro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abbatiale_Saint-Pierre_de_Corbie,_int%C3%A9rieur,_Adh%C3%A9lard_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
